{
  "extends": "survive.jsonc",
  "name": "Castle Victoria and graveyard shift",
  "why": "hold Castle Victoria on its skeleton generator, with the Tos graveyard worked during its night window, blunt weapons first, and roaming off",

  "claim": {
    "movement": "bot",
    "work": "bot",
    "economy": "bot",
    "lease_ms": 600000
  },

  "cadence": {
    "character_ms": 30000,
    "fleet_ms": 120000,
    "backoff_ms": 60000
  },

  "strategies": {
    "enabled": true,
    // `vs-skeletons` is deliberately gone and `short-swording` replaces it. They are both
    // weapon orders and a unit holding both would be told two things; short swording wins
    // the tie in code, but selecting both would still be a doctrine that cannot say what
    // it wants.
    //
    // `check-cv-crate` is gone with the rest of the Castle Victoria work: the crate is
    // under Castle Victoria and this fleet is no longer anywhere near it. Leaving it
    // selected would send whichever unit came up in the rotation on a walk across the
    // world and back, for a crate whose expected value is about forty shillings.
    // EVERY TOWN TRIP CROSSES UKGOTH, SO THERE ARE NO TOWN TRIPS.
    //
    // Room 599, Ukgoth, Holy Land of Trolls: troll level 90, attack rating 750. It sits on
    // the route from Castle Victoria to BOTH banks — 38->54 goes 2, 599, 598, 597, 596, 61
    // and 38->376 goes 2, 599, 589, 579, 578 — and the danger-aware router already returns
    // the safest of those. There is no way round it.
    //
    // A level-90 troll is ABOVE this fleet's band. Max healths are 53-56 now, so the
    // engagement ceiling is 79-84 and `refuseEngagement` refuses the fight: a character
    // meeting one can only run, and travel doctrine is that a planned trip is completed
    // through whatever attacks it. Measured 2026-08-14: ten deaths in one half hour, SEVEN
    // of them in Ukgoth, and three characters died there more than once in that window —
    // one of them three times.
    //
    // So the selling, banking and restocking strategies are off. The fleet keeps what it
    // kills until its pack fills and then drops the cheapest thing, which costs shillings;
    // crossing Ukgoth twice a trip costs characters, and this fleet has already fallen
    // from 60-61 max health to 53-56. Create Food covers vigor from reagents already
    // carried, so nothing here depends on a shop.
    //
    // Turn these back on when the fleet can fight a troll — that is 60 max health for the
    // ceiling to reach 90 — or when it works somewhere with a road that avoids 599.
    "defaults": ["create-weapons", "vs-skeletons", "create-food", "max-weapons", "accumulate-in-vault", "farm-cleanup"],
    "settings": {
      "sell-and-bank": {
        "bank_above": 3000,
        "walking_money": 1000,
        "max_carry": 50,
        "sell_at_load": 0.95,
        "sell_when_broke": false,
        "broke_under": 500,
        "broke_stacks": 8
      },
      "accumulate-in-vault": {
        "items": [
          "dark angel feather",
          "Inky-cap mushroom",
          "blue dragon scale",
          "arrows",
          "nerudite arrows"
        ]
      }
    }
  },

  // CASTLE VICTORIA IS THE PRIMARY, and the detour through 2601 is why it is stated
  // twice. `huntingGrounds` ranks 2601 top for skeletons — 80% at a shared cap of 25
  // against 38's 40% at a cap of 10 — so the keeper kept walking back to it and the
  // doctrine kept sending units to 38, with the fleet parked on safe walls in between.
  //
  // The keeper's ranking was reading the spawn table, and the spawn table describes what
  // a room GENERATES rather than what is standing in it. 2601 has 26 statues in it, 23 of
  // which expose no `attack` verb at all: unkillable from the wire, counted as monsters by
  // the server, holding the room permanently over its cap. It generates nothing and never
  // will. It is now out of HUNT_ROOMS entirely, because anything allowed to consider it
  // will keep choosing it.
  //
    // THE KING'S WAY IS GONE. It cost 69 deaths in a day —
  // 23 at the destination to frogmen, which swarm to a cap of 9, and 32 more in the troll
  // corridors it can only be reached through (36 kills by trolls, more than the frogmen
  // managed). Neither half was survivable at this fleet's size and the two compounded:
  // every trip out and back crossed rating-750 rooms to reach a room that ganged up.
  //
  // The stations are ordered by preference and a unit takes the first its ENGAGEMENT
  // CEILING admits — `refuseEngagement` refuses a creature above round(max_health * 1.5),
  // and it gates the whole room, not just the quarry. That single rule does the sorting
  // the operator asked for without a health threshold written anywhere:
  //
  //     >= 50 max health  ->  ceiling >= 75  ->  38 Castle Victoria, skeletons
  //     40 to 49          ->  ceiling 60-74  ->  39 Upstairs, battered skeletons
  //     under 40          ->  544 Valley of Ileria, fungus beasts
  //
  // A HEALTH NUMBER IN THE DOCTRINE WOULD BE A SECOND ANSWER TO THAT QUESTION, and this
  // repository's whole history is quantities with two homes disagreeing. The ceiling is
  // already the thing the keeper enforces; duplicating it here as "> 60" would also have
  // selected NOBODY, because a day in the King's Way took the whole fleet under 61.
  //
  // 39 CARRIES AN EXPLICIT SHARE OF 1.0 SO IT TAKES THE CASTLE'S OVERFLOW, and 544 —
  // being last — would otherwise absorb it: the allocator hands the final station whatever
  // is left so that rounding cannot lose a unit, which is right until the final station is
  // a floor nobody should be sent to. With 39 claiming everything it can admit, 544's pool
  // is exactly the units 39 refused.
  //
  // 544 IS THE FLOOR AND IT IS THERE FOR THE CASUALTIES. A day in the King's Way took one
  // character from 53 max health to 36, and at 36 the ceiling is 54 — below the zombies
  // upstairs, so the castle has nothing it may fight. A fungus beast is rating 210, the
  // softest fight in the game, and still level 50, so the kills advance it back up. It is
  // last, so nobody who can work the castle is sent there.
  //
  // 70 is first so the night window gets its pick while it is open — see below.
  "shift": {
    "on": true,
    "stations": [
      // THE GRAVEYARD IS OFF. Measured 2026-08-14: it costs two migrations per two-hour
      // cycle and EVERY road out of Castle Victoria crosses rating-750 troll country —
      // 38 -> 70 is eight hops through 599/598/597, and back again. The window itself is
      // productive, but the fleet was doing 210 kills a half hour parked in the castle and
      // 39 in the half hour it spent walking to and from the graveyard, with three deaths
      // in rooms that are neither station.
      //
      // The room is also 85% zombie and 15% skeleton, so the thing being walked to is a
      // WORSE skeleton generator than the one being left: 38 is 40% skeleton, continuous,
      // and zero hops away. Re-enable this only if the corridor gets safer or the fleet
      // gets big enough to survive it.
      // 1.0 HERE MEANT "EVERYONE WHO CAN FIGHT A SKELETON", AND THAT IS SEVENTEEN OF
      // TWENTY-ONE IN ONE ROOM. `share` is a fraction of the ELIGIBLE units and the last
      // station takes the remainder, so downstairs took all comers and upstairs got only
      // the four characters too small to be eligible at all — the ones whose engagement
      // ceiling (1.5x max health) falls under a skeleton's level 75.
      //
      // Measured 2026-08-14, over one 30-minute window:
      //
      //   room 38, skeleton     L75 rating 525   17 characters   2.9 kills each   6 deaths
      //   room 39, batt. skel.  L60 rating 420    4 characters   8.8 kills each   0 deaths
      //
      // Three times the yield per character and nobody killed. Two things drive it and
      // they compound: a level-75 skeleton at rating 525 is a hard fight for a roster of
      // 45-62 max health, and `piMonster_count_max` IS ROOM-WIDE — seventeen characters
      // in one room are competing for one capped generator, so most of them are standing
      // about waiting for something to spawn.
      //
      // 0.6 moved seven upstairs, and the next window SETTLED IT — in the other direction
      // from the guess above. The worry was that room 39 would reproduce downstairs'
      // crowding once it filled up. It did not:
      //
      //   room 39   4 characters -> 8.8 each        11 characters -> 8.7 each, 0 deaths
      //   room 38  17 characters -> 2.9 each        10 characters -> 1.6 each, 8 deaths
      //
      // Room 39 HELD ITS RATE at nearly three times the occupancy, so the shared generator
      // is not what was binding. And room 38 got WORSE per character after being thinned,
      // which kills the crowding explanation for it too: the problem is the QUARRY. A
      // level-75 skeleton at rating 525 is simply a bad fight for a roster of 45-62 max
      // health, and it produced eight of the last twelve deaths while 39 produced none.
      //
      // So 0.25 — but NOT for the reason first written here, which was wrong and is worth
      // recording. That comment said a quarter leaves the LARGEST characters downstairs,
      // where they belong, because advancement wants the creature's level strictly above
      // max health and a level-60 battered skeleton stops paying a character that has
      // reached 60. The reasoning about advancement is right. The mechanism is not:
      // `pickOrder(rows, false)` RETURNS THE ROWS UNSORTED, so a non-lead station takes
      // them in roster order and the split is arbitrary with respect to level. Checked
      // live the moment it deployed: the seven left downstairs measured 50, 50, 52, 53,
      // 53, 56 and 56 max health, while the three biggest in the fleet — 62, 61 and 61 —
      // all went upstairs. The opposite of the intent.
      //
      // What 0.25 actually buys, then, is only that MOST of the fleet works the softer
      // quarry. Seven characters are still on the level-75 skeleton by accident of roster
      // order rather than because they are the ones who need it.
      //
      // A share cannot express "the largest stay". `max` is a capacity and beats the share
      // but is just as blind to level; doing this properly needs the station list to select
      // on level, which is a code change and not a doctrine one. Until then do not read
      // this number as targeting anybody in particular.
      //
      // Both rooms are Castle Victoria, one floor apart. This moves nobody through a
      // corridor.
      // AND NOW SHUT, because at a quarter it was costing lives to produce nothing.
      // Measured over the following 30 minutes: room 38 held four characters, returned
      // TWO kills between them, and produced three of the fleet's last six deaths. Room 39
      // over the same span returned 8.9 per character and killed nobody — its fourth
      // consecutive reading in that band, now across occupancies of 4, 11, 18 and 17.
      //
      // A share of 0 empties the station (checked: the allocation returns 21 upstairs, and
      // the `|| rows.length` fallback that would have inverted a zero lives on a different
      // path).
      //
      // WHAT THIS GIVES UP, PLAINLY: advancement wants the creature's level STRICTLY above
      // max health, so a level-60 battered skeleton stops paying anyone who reaches 60 and
      // this closes the fleet's only route past it. That is a real cost and it is deferred,
      // not solved. It is affordable today only because the three characters at 61-62 were
      // already upstairs where the level-75 skeleton could not help them — the split is
      // roster-ordered, not level-ordered, so room 38 was never serving the characters that
      // needed it. Re-open it the moment the station list can select on level.
      { "room": 38, "hunt": "skeleton", "share": 0 },
      { "room": 39, "hunt": "battered skeleton", "share": 1.0 },
      { "room": 544, "hunt": "fungus beast" }
    ],
    "rest_below": 0.75,
    "flee_below": 0.35,
    // THE FLEET HAS NO HERBS, AND THAT IS STILL NOT WHY IT RESTS. Measured 2026-08-14:
    // herbs were ZERO on all 21 characters over two check-ins half an hour apart, and
    // elderberry was 10 in the whole fleet. `create food` costs 2 of each, so nothing
    // could cast it and vigor cannot pass the rest threshold of 80 by resting alone.
    //
    // I dropped this to 60 on the first of those check-ins, reasoning that a floor of
    // 180 could never be cleared and so was parking the eight characters then sitting at
    // exactly v80. THAT WAS WRONG and is reverted. The change never went live — DUM was
    // not restarted — yet the next reading had three characters at v72-v78 hunting
    // with the floor still at 180. Characters below the floor fight. Whatever
    // parks them, it is not this number, and `rest_below: 0.75` is the likelier gate.
    //
    // Keep the herb-zero finding, which is real and unfixed; discard the inference drawn
    // from it. Do not re-lower this without a reading that shows the floor itself
    // refusing a fight.
    "fight_above_vigor": 180,
    "use_safe_spots": true
  },

  // EVERY CASTLE VICTORIA EFFORT IS OFF, and all three of these have to be off together.
  // The shift assigns rooms 38/39, the crate walks a unit to room 41, and the placement
  // spread pins the same two castle rooms — leaving any one of them on would drag part of
  // the fleet back across the world while the crypt rule sent it the other way, and the
  // two would fight every tick.
  "castle_victoria": { "shift": false },
  "crate": { "check": false },
  "placement": { "spread": false, "rooms": [] },

  "weapons": {
    "preset": "vsSkeletons",
    "provision": {
      "enabled": false,
      "threshold": "axe",
      "room": null,
      "staging_only": false,
      "cast_when_mana": true,
      "mana_cost": 15
    }
  },

  "food": {
    "min_items": 1,
    "mana_cost": 10,
    "elderberry_per_cast": 2,
    "herbs_per_cast": 2
  },

  "record": {
    "full_observations": false
  },

  "graveyard": { "shift": false },
  "prey": { "choose": false, "flee_below": 0.35 }
}
